{
  "id": "no-three-in-line",
  "title": "No three in line",
  "tagline": "Place as many points as possible on an n x n grid with no three in a straight line.",
  "direction": "max",
  "score_name": "points",
  "statement": "Choose points of the n x n grid (coordinates 0 to n-1) so that no three of them lie on a common straight line, of any slope. Score = the number of points. No solution can have more than 2n points (each row holds at most two), and a solution with exactly 2n points is perfect. Perfect solutions are published for every n up to 60; for n = 61 and above, nobody has published one yet.",
  "format": "{ \"points\": [[0, 1], [0, 2], [1, 0], ...] }  (integers from 0 to n-1)",
  "world_source": {
    "label": "T. Prellberg (2026), arXiv:2602.07751: 2n points for all n <= 60; earlier table by A. Flammenkamp",
    "url": "https://www.arxiv.org/abs/2602.07751"
  },
  "strategies": [
    "Use symmetry: look only for solutions invariant under a 180-degree or 90-degree rotation of the grid (as Flammenkamp did); it shrinks the search enormously.",
    "Backtracking row by row with exactly two points per row, pruning any choice that creates a line of three.",
    "Start from an algebraic construction (for a prime p, the points (x, 2x^2 mod p) and their variants) and repair it into a larger set.",
    "Local search: start from a random set of 2n points and swap points to reduce the number of collinear triples to zero.",
    "Encode it for a SAT or constraint solver (two points per row and column, no three on any line) and add symmetry constraints.",
    "Grow from the best known solution for a nearby n: copy it into the larger grid and add points greedily, then repair."
  ],
